        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm wondering whether a model needs a root joint to work properly, so I'll set up one root joint at the origin and attach parts to it, maybe with hinges for a hanging sign or book lid. I'm picturing a tall wizard's bookshelf with an elegant arched silhouette, ornate gold trim, deep navy and warm wood tones, and teal-violet glow accents, sized around 2.2 meters tall.\n\nThinking through floor clutter too—a stack of books, candle cluster, a scroll basket—alongside the shelf structure itself: side panels with arched, finialed tops, a plinth base, a back panel, five shelf levels, and a gothic crown with filigree, a crescent moon, and stars. For the books, I'm planning around sixty individual parts, each built from a cover box, an inset cream page block, and a gold spine band, balancing visual richness against the part budget.\n\nTo keep part counts manageable while still allowing many books, I'll merge geometry by category: covers merged per colour group, pages merged as cream, gold bands merged together, and label plates merged too, with leaning books achieved by transforming the geometry matrix rather than adding rotated parts. The model faces +Z with book spines forward; I'm laying out the shelf with roughly 1.1 m inner width, 0.36 m depth, and an overall height near 2.55 m including a plinth base, five shelf boards creating four tiers, and a pointed arch crown on top. For the bottom tier, I'm placing oversized tomes (including a chained one) and a globe on a stand to anchor the composition.\n\nI'm filling the remaining tiers with wizard paraphernalia: tier two gets standing books, a glowing orb on a brass stand, and a candle; tier three has a diamond-cubby scroll rack, stacked books, an hourglass, quill and inkwell, potion vials, and a small crystal cluster; tier four adds more books plus a dripping candelabra and an armillary sphere acting as a star-chart orb. On top I'm adding the arch crown, a candelabra, and a wizard's hat for charm, plus a hinged hanging star charm on a chain for a small moving-part detail.\n\nI want a floating glowing orb with rotating rings hovering just above the crown, using hinge joints so the rings can spin, keeping the whole thing anime-appropriate but still visually connected to the structure. On the floor I'm adding a stack of books with a melted candle on one side and a basket of scrolls on the other, bringing the total footprint to roughly two meters wide, and I'm thinking through how to render the candles with wax drips and glowing flame halos, possibly using a radial gradient card for the glow effect.\n\nFor the flame itself I'll skip the halo card approach since alphaTest would make the gradient look like a hard disc, and instead layer two lathe shapes—an outer orange glow and inner yellow-white core—both with emissive glow. For the wood I'm applying cel-shaded grain painting, letting shadows fall naturally from a key light positioned up-front-left, and I'm calculating texel density across roughly 25 square meters of shelf surface to decide on a 2048px paint atlas for crisp detail versus flat color blocks.\n\nBooks get their cel-painted covers using the merged geometry's UV coordinates, which works fine even though painting many small items will dilute the texel density across the shared paint sheet. For surface decoration I'm planning SVG-based texture strips—starting with a gold filigree frieze of vines and stars rendered on a transparent plane mounted slightly in front of the wood surface so only the gold linework appears to float over the grain.\n\nSpine decals follow the same transparent-background approach: a handful of SVG spine designs (banded gold stripes, rune circles, title plates, stars) get stretched onto planes sized to each book, with grayscale art tinted per-color to keep atlas variants cheap. I'm now weighing whether to keep books merged or render them as individual parts for more flexible per-book texturing.\n\nWait, there's a problem with merging boxes